From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Heinemeier Hansson <david@hey.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 13:50:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qcom: x1e80100: raise the Zenbook A16 digital speaker
 ceiling to 0 dB

The upstream -3 dB digital cap holds a full-scale signal 3 dB short of
the WSA884x amplifiers' full scale. On the ASUS Zenbook A16 (UX3607OA)
the speakers were quiet even at 100% volume, and raising the WSA RX
Digital Volume cap to 84 (0 dB), the value the upstream UCM requests,
gave the full 3 dB: a woofer measured with the built-in microphones
tracked its input dB for dB up to 0 dBFS at 0.4% THD (600 Hz), and a
tweeter stayed under 2% at -3 dBFS (5 kHz).

Its PA Volume is left at the upstream 0 dB cap: on this board the
control does not change the output at all (the same level at PA 6 and
12, with the amplifier's compander on and off, on fresh streams), so a
higher cap would buy nothing.
---
 sound/soc/qcom/x1e80100.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sound/soc/qcom/x1e80100.c b/sound/soc/qcom/x1e80100.c
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/x1e80100.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/x1e80100.c
@@ -47,10 +47,18 @@
 		 * cap left its speakers at roughly half the Windows loudness;
 		 * its amplifiers' OCP, PBR and thermal protection (the wsa884x
 		 * 2S/4 ohm profile) are relied on instead.
+		 *
+		 * The ASUS Zenbook A16 gets the 0 dB digital ceiling only. Its
+		 * PA Volume does not change the output, so raising that cap
+		 * would buy nothing; the digital cap was what held a full-scale
+		 * signal 3 dB short of the amplifiers' full scale.
 		 */
 		if (of_machine_is_compatible("microsoft,denali-oled")) {
 			digital_max = 84;
 			pa_max = 12;
+		} else if (of_machine_is_compatible("asus,zenbook-a16-ux3607oa")) {
+			digital_max = 84;
+			pa_max = 6;
 		} else {
 			digital_max = 81;
 			pa_max = 6;
